(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("virtualization",{"virtualization/what-virtualization-is-and-hypervisor-types":{"html":"\u003cp>A server with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB of memory usually runs far below its limits when it hosts one application. Virtualization lets one physical machine, the host, run many virtual machines, called guests, each with its own operating system, and shares the hardware between them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a hypervisor does and why modern CPUs help it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Type 1 and type 2 hypervisors, with KVM, ESXi, Hyper-V and Proxmox as examples\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a virtual machine differs from a container\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The pieces of a KVM host: kernel module, QEMU, libvirt\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-hypervisor-does\">What a hypervisor does\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>hypervisor\u003c/strong> is the software that creates virtual machines and hands each one a share of CPU, memory, disk and network. A guest believes it owns a whole computer. The hypervisor keeps guests apart, so a crash or an attack inside one guest does not reach the others.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Fast virtualization depends on \u003cstrong>hardware support\u003c/strong>. On Intel this is VT-x, on AMD it is AMD-V. These CPU features let guest code run directly on the processor, and only a small set of sensitive operations is trapped by the hypervisor. Without them, virtualization is slow or needs tricks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -E -o 'vmx|svm' /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u\nlsmod | grep kvm\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -E -o \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;vmx|svm&#x27;\u003c/span> /proc/cpuinfo | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -u\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vmx\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsmod | grep kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kvm_intel             380928  0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">kvm                  1142784  1 kvm_intel\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>vmx\u003c/code> means Intel VT-x is present and \u003ccode>svm\u003c/code> means AMD-V. If neither appears, the feature may be off in the firmware settings, or you are already inside a virtual machine that does not pass it through.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"type-1-and-type-2\">Type 1 and type 2\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Runs on\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical use\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Type 1 (bare metal)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Directly on hardware\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen, Proxmox VE\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Servers and private clouds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Type 2 (hosted)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inside a normal operating system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Laptops, testing\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The line is not perfectly sharp. \u003cstrong>KVM\u003c/strong> is a part of the Linux kernel, so Linux itself becomes the hypervisor, which is why KVM is usually counted as type 1. \u003cstrong>Proxmox VE\u003c/strong> is a Debian-based system that bundles KVM virtual machines, containers, clustering and a web interface.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-kvm-stack\">The KVM stack\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>KVM\u003c/strong> is the kernel module that uses the CPU features.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>QEMU\u003c/strong> is the user-space program that emulates the rest of the machine: devices, disks, network cards, and it is the process you see for each guest.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>libvirt\u003c/strong> is the management layer. Tools such as \u003ccode>virsh\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>virt-manager\u003c/code> talk to libvirt, which starts QEMU with the right options.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>virtio\u003c/strong> is a family of paravirtual drivers. A guest with virtio disk and network drivers is much faster than one that uses emulated hardware.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh list --all\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh list --all\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Id   Name        State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> 3    web-01      running\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> -    batch-02    shut off\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-machines-and-containers\">Virtual machines and containers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A virtual machine includes a full guest kernel. A container shares the host kernel and isolates processes with namespaces and cgroups. That makes containers lighter and faster to start, and virtual machines a stronger boundary because an attacker has to escape a hypervisor, not just a shared kernel. Many platforms run containers inside virtual machines to get both.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-guest-that-would-not-start\">Incident: The guest that would not start\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new KVM host refused to start any guest and the team assumed the image was broken.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh start web-01\nlsmod | grep kvm\ngrep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh start web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: Failed to start domain 'web-01'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: unsupported configuration: Domain requires KVM, but it is not available. Check that virtualization is enabled in the host BIOS, and host configuration is setup to load the kvm modules.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>lsmod | grep kvm\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The image was fine. Hardware virtualization was switched off in the server firmware, so the CPU did not advertise \u003ccode>vmx\u003c/code> and the kvm module could not load. After the setting was enabled and the host restarted, the same command worked. When a guest will not start, check the host before the guest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Judging a host by CPU use alone and ignoring memory, which is usually the first limit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running guests on emulated disk and network devices instead of virtio\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that nested virtualization (a hypervisor inside a guest) needs the setting enabled on the outer host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a virtual machine as a security boundary that needs no patching\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-hypervisor-does","text":"What a hypervisor does"},{"id":"type-1-and-type-2","text":"Type 1 and type 2"},{"id":"the-kvm-stack","text":"The KVM stack"},{"id":"virtual-machines-and-containers","text":"Virtual machines and containers"},{"id":"incident-the-guest-that-would-not-start","text":"Incident: The guest that would not start"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KVM documentation","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Documentation"},{"t":"libvirt documentation","u":"https://libvirt.org/docs.html"},{"t":"QEMU documentation","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/"}],"text":"A server with 64 CPU cores and 512 GB of memory usually runs far below its limits when it hosts one application. Virtualization lets one physical machine, the host, run many virtual machines, called guests, each with its own operating system, and shares the hardware between them. What you will learn What a hypervisor does and why modern CPUs help it Type 1 and type 2 hypervisors, with KVM, ESXi, Hyper-V and Proxmox as examples How a virtual machine differs from a container The pieces of a KVM host: kernel module, QEMU, libvirt What a hypervisor does A hypervisor is the software that creates virtual machines and hands each one a share of CPU, memory, disk and network. A guest believes it owns a whole computer. The hypervisor keeps guests apart, so a crash or an attack inside one guest does not reach the others. Fast virtualization depends on hardware support . On Intel this is VT-x, on AMD it is AMD-V. These CPU features let guest code run directly on the processor, and only a small set of sensitive operations is trapped by the hypervisor. Without them, virtualization is slow or needs tricks. $ grep -E -o &#x27;vmx|svm&#x27; /proc/cpuinfo | sort -u vmx $ lsmod | grep kvm kvm_intel 380928 0 kvm 1142784 1 kvm_intel vmx means Intel VT-x is present and svm means AMD-V. If neither appears, the feature may be off in the firmware settings, or you are already inside a virtual machine that does not pass it through. Type 1 and type 2 Kind Runs on Examples Typical use Type 1 (bare metal) Directly on hardware VMware ESXi, Microsoft Hyper-V, KVM, Xen, Proxmox VE Servers and private clouds Type 2 (hosted) Inside a normal operating system VirtualBox, VMware Workstation, Parallels Laptops, testing The line is not perfectly sharp. KVM is a part of the Linux kernel, so Linux itself becomes the hypervisor, which is why KVM is usually counted as type 1. Proxmox VE is a Debian-based system that bundles KVM virtual machines, containers, clustering and a web interface. The KVM stack KVM is the kernel module that uses the CPU features. QEMU is the user-space program that emulates the rest of the machine: devices, disks, network cards, and it is the process you see for each guest. libvirt is the management layer. Tools such as virsh and virt-manager talk to libvirt, which starts QEMU with the right options. virtio is a family of paravirtual drivers. A guest with virtio disk and network drivers is much faster than one that uses emulated hardware. $ virsh list --all Id Name State ------------------------------ 3 web-01 running - batch-02 shut off Virtual machines and containers A virtual machine includes a full guest kernel. A container shares the host kernel and isolates processes with namespaces and cgroups. That makes containers lighter and faster to start, and virtual machines a stronger boundary because an attacker has to escape a hypervisor, not just a shared kernel. Many platforms run containers inside virtual machines to get both. Incident: The guest that would not start Incident A new KVM host refused to start any guest and the team assumed the image was broken. $ virsh start web-01 error: Failed to start domain 'web-01' error: unsupported configuration: Domain requires KVM, but it is not available. Check that virtualization is enabled in the host BIOS, and host configuration is setup to load the kvm modules. $ lsmod | grep kvm $ grep -c vmx /proc/cpuinfo 0 The image was fine. Hardware virtualization was switched off in the server firmware, so the CPU did not advertise vmx and the kvm module could not load. After the setting was enabled and the host restarted, the same command worked. When a guest will not start, check the host before the guest. Common mistakes Judging a host by CPU use alone and ignoring memory, which is usually the first limit Running guests on emulated disk and network devices instead of virtio Forgetting that nested virtualization (a hypervisor inside a guest) needs the setting enabled on the outer host Treating a virtual ma"},"virtualization/cpu-memory-and-overcommit":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud and private cloud hosts rarely give every guest dedicated hardware. They promise more virtual CPUs and memory than exist, and rely on guests not asking for it all at once. That is \u003cstrong>overcommit\u003c/strong>. It saves money until the day it does not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How virtual CPUs are scheduled onto physical cores\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Memory overcommit, ballooning, KSM and swapping\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>CPU ready time and steal time, the signs of a crowded host\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NUMA and why a large guest can run slower than a small one\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"virtual-cpus\">Virtual CPUs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>vCPU\u003c/strong> is a thread of the host that runs a guest&#39;s CPU. If a host has 16 cores (32 with hyper-threading) and the guests together have 80 vCPUs, the ratio is 2.5 vCPUs per thread. Light workloads often tolerate 4 to 1 or more, busy production databases need close to 1 to 1.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>When more vCPUs want to run than there are free threads, some wait. The guest sees this as \u003cstrong>steal time\u003c/strong>, shown as \u003ccode>st\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>top\u003c/code> inside a Linux guest. VMware shows the same idea as \u003cstrong>CPU ready\u003c/strong>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"top -bn1 | head -4 | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>top -bn1 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">%Cpu(s): 22.0 us,  6.0 sy,  0.0 ni, 54.0 id,  0.0 wa,  0.0 hi,  0.0 si, 18.0 st\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Eighteen percent steal means the guest wanted CPU and the host could not give it. The guest looks idle at 54 percent, yet it is slow. The fix is on the host: move guests away, reduce vCPU counts, or add hardware.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"memory\">Memory\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Memory is the harder resource, because a guest that is out of memory fails instead of slowing down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Ballooning\u003c/strong> lets the host ask a guest driver to take memory back. The guest swaps or drops cache to comply.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>KSM (kernel samepage merging)\u003c/strong> shares identical pages between guests. It saves memory and costs some CPU.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Host swap\u003c/strong> is the last resort. A guest whose memory is swapped by the host becomes very slow, because the guest does not know.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Huge pages\u003c/strong> reduce overhead for large guests such as databases.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"free -m\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>free -m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">               total        used        free      shared  buff/cache   available\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Mem:          262144      249910        1210         312       11024        9800\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Swap:          32768       14200       18568\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>When the host itself starts using swap with guests running, memory is overcommitted too far. A common rule is to keep memory overcommit near 1 to 1 for production, and to allow more only where guests are idle and can be paused.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"numa\">NUMA\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Large servers have several CPU sockets, each with memory attached. Reading memory from another socket is slower. A guest bigger than one socket may span both and lose speed. Where possible, pin large guests to one NUMA node, or size them to fit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-monday-morning-slowdown\">Incident: The Monday morning slowdown\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every Monday at 09:00 the web guests slowed, yet their graphs showed low CPU.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">guest$ top -bn1 | grep Cpu\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">%Cpu(s): 15.0 us,  4.0 sy,  0.0 ni, 40.0 id,  0.0 wa,  0.0 hi,  0.0 si, 41.0 st\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ virsh nodeinfo | grep -E 'CPU\\(s\\)|Memory'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">CPU(s):              32\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Memory size:         268435456 KiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ virsh list | wc -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">39\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Forty-one percent steal showed the guests were waiting for the host. Thirty-six running guests shared 32 threads, and a weekly batch of reports started at 09:00 on the same host. The team moved the batch guests to a separate host and set a limit of 3 vCPUs per thread for that cluster. Guest graphs inside the guest can hide this problem. Steal time and host CPU tell the truth.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Give every VM 16 vCPUs so none of them is ever short of CPU.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># 20 guests, each with &lt;vcpu&gt;16&lt;/vcpu&gt; on a 32-core host\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ top\n%Cpu(s): ... 31.4 st\n# guests report high load and slow responses\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> Many vCPUs make the scheduler find many free cores at once, so a guest with 16 vCPUs waits longer and steal time rises for everybody. More vCPUs is not more speed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode># start small and measure\n&lt;vcpu&gt;2&lt;/vcpu&gt;\n# watch steal time\nvmstat 5 3\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Size guests to their real needs, increase only when the guest is CPU bound, and watch steal time.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Giving every guest many vCPUs &quot;to be safe&quot;: more vCPUs can make scheduling worse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommitting memory on a host that runs databases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only guest CPU graphs and ignoring steal or ready time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting a guest larger than a NUMA node without pinning\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"virtual-cpus","text":"Virtual CPUs"},{"id":"memory","text":"Memory"},{"id":"numa","text":"NUMA"},{"id":"incident-the-monday-morning-slowdown","text":"Incident: The Monday morning slowdown"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"KVM: Documentation","u":"https://www.linux-kvm.org/page/Documentation"},{"t":"Linux kernel: KSM","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/admin-guide/mm/ksm.html"},{"t":"libvirt: NUMA tuning","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatdomain.html#numa-node-tuning"}],"text":"Cloud and private cloud hosts rarely give every guest dedicated hardware. They promise more virtual CPUs and memory than exist, and rely on guests not asking for it all at once. That is overcommit . It saves money until the day it does not. What you will learn How virtual CPUs are scheduled onto physical cores Memory overcommit, ballooning, KSM and swapping CPU ready time and steal time, the signs of a crowded host NUMA and why a large guest can run slower than a small one Virtual CPUs A vCPU is a thread of the host that runs a guest&#39;s CPU. If a host has 16 cores (32 with hyper-threading) and the guests together have 80 vCPUs, the ratio is 2.5 vCPUs per thread. Light workloads often tolerate 4 to 1 or more, busy production databases need close to 1 to 1. When more vCPUs want to run than there are free threads, some wait. The guest sees this as steal time , shown as st in top inside a Linux guest. VMware shows the same idea as CPU ready . $ top -bn1 | head -4 | tail -2 %Cpu(s): 22.0 us, 6.0 sy, 0.0 ni, 54.0 id, 0.0 wa, 0.0 hi, 0.0 si, 18.0 st Eighteen percent steal means the guest wanted CPU and the host could not give it. The guest looks idle at 54 percent, yet it is slow. The fix is on the host: move guests away, reduce vCPU counts, or add hardware. Memory Memory is the harder resource, because a guest that is out of memory fails instead of slowing down. Ballooning lets the host ask a guest driver to take memory back. The guest swaps or drops cache to comply. KSM (kernel samepage merging) shares identical pages between guests. It saves memory and costs some CPU. Host swap is the last resort. A guest whose memory is swapped by the host becomes very slow, because the guest does not know. Huge pages reduce overhead for large guests such as databases. $ free -m total used free shared buff/cache available Mem: 262144 249910 1210 312 11024 9800 Swap: 32768 14200 18568 When the host itself starts using swap with guests running, memory is overcommitted too far. A common rule is to keep memory overcommit near 1 to 1 for production, and to allow more only where guests are idle and can be paused. NUMA Large servers have several CPU sockets, each with memory attached. Reading memory from another socket is slower. A guest bigger than one socket may span both and lose speed. Where possible, pin large guests to one NUMA node, or size them to fit. Incident: The Monday morning slowdown Incident Every Monday at 09:00 the web guests slowed, yet their graphs showed low CPU. guest$ top -bn1 | grep Cpu %Cpu(s): 15.0 us, 4.0 sy, 0.0 ni, 40.0 id, 0.0 wa, 0.0 hi, 0.0 si, 41.0 st host$ virsh nodeinfo | grep -E 'CPU\\(s\\)|Memory' CPU(s): 32 Memory size: 268435456 KiB host$ virsh list | wc -l 39 Forty-one percent steal showed the guests were waiting for the host. Thirty-six running guests shared 32 threads, and a weekly batch of reports started at 09:00 on the same host. The team moved the batch guests to a separate host and set a limit of 3 vCPUs per thread for that cluster. Guest graphs inside the guest can hide this problem. Steal time and host CPU tell the truth. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Give every VM 16 vCPUs so none of them is ever short of CPU. The tempting way # 20 guests, each with \u003cvcpu>16\u003c/vcpu> on a 32-core host See what goes wrong $ top %Cpu(s): ... 31.4 st # guests report high load and slow responses Why it fails. Many vCPUs make the scheduler find many free cores at once, so a guest with 16 vCPUs waits longer and steal time rises for everybody. More vCPUs is not more speed. See the way that works The way that works # start small and measure \u003cvcpu>2\u003c/vcpu> # watch steal time vmstat 5 3 Size guests to their real needs, increase only when the guest is CPU bound, and watch steal time. Common mistakes Giving every guest many vCPUs \"to be safe\": more vCPUs can make scheduling worse Overcommitting memory on a host that runs databases Reading only guest CPU graphs and ignoring steal or ready time Starting a guest larger than"},"virtualization/virtual-disks-snapshots-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>A virtual machine&#39;s disk is a file or a volume on the host. How that file is stored decides speed, space use and what happens when you take a snapshot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Common image formats: raw, qcow2, VMDK, VHDX\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Thin and thick provisioning\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a snapshot works and why long chains cause trouble\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Templates, clones and cloud-init\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"image-formats\">Image formats\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Format\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Used by\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Notes\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>raw\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>KVM, many clouds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simple and fast, no features of its own\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>qcow2\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>KVM and QEMU\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thin provisioning, snapshots, compression\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>VMDK\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>VMware\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thin or thick, snapshots as delta files\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>VHDX\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hyper-V\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Dynamic or fixed\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"qemu-img info web-01.qcow2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>qemu-img info web-01.qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">image: web-01.qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">file format: qcow2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">virtual size: 100 GiB (107374182400 bytes)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">disk size: 8.2 GiB\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>virtual size\u003c/strong> is what the guest sees. The \u003cstrong>disk size\u003c/strong> is what the file takes on the host. That gap is \u003cstrong>thin provisioning\u003c/strong>: space is taken only as the guest writes. It saves space and lets you promise more than you have, which is another kind of overcommit. If every guest fills its disk, the storage pool runs out and guests fail with write errors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"snapshots\">Snapshots\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A snapshot freezes the disk at a point in time. New writes go to a separate file, called a delta or overlay, and the original becomes read-only. Rolling back means discarding the delta.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade\nvirsh snapshot-list web-01\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Domain snapshot before-upgrade created\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list web-01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Name             Creation Time               State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">------------------------------------------------------\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> before-upgrade   2026-11-02 10:14:09 +0000   shutoff\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A snapshot is not a backup. It lives on the same storage as the disk, so losing the storage loses both. Each snapshot adds a layer: reads may have to look through several files, which slows the guest, and every delta uses space. A \u003cstrong>chain\u003c/strong> of dozens of snapshots can fill a pool and make deleting them slow because deltas must be merged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Use snapshots for short-lived safety, such as before an upgrade, and delete them when the change is accepted.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-and-clones\">Templates and clones\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>template\u003c/strong> is a prepared guest image with the operating system installed and cleaned. A \u003cstrong>full clone\u003c/strong> copies the disk. A \u003cstrong>linked clone\u003c/strong> uses the template as a read-only base and stores only the differences, so it is fast and small, but depends on the base.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>cloud-init\u003c/strong> configures a guest at first boot from user data: host name, users, SSH keys, packages. It lets one generic template serve many roles.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pool-that-filled-overnight\">Incident: The pool that filled overnight\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pool that was 60 percent full on Friday reached 100 percent on Sunday and every guest paused.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh pool-info vmstore\nvirsh snapshot-list db-01 | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh pool-info vmstore\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Capacity:       500.00 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Allocation:     499.98 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Available:      0.02 GiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh snapshot-list db-01 | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">42\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A backup tool created a snapshot of \u003ccode>db-01\u003c/code> every hour and never removed them. Forty snapshots each held the changes of an hour of database writes. Deleting the old snapshots merged the files and freed 180 GiB, and the guests resumed. The team added a retention rule and an alert at 80 percent pool use.\u003c/p>\n\u003csection class=\"wp\" aria-label=\"Worked example with a wrong path\">\u003ch4 class=\"wp-h\">Worked example: a tempting wrong path\u003c/h4>\u003cp class=\"wp-goal\">\u003cstrong>Goal.\u003c/strong> Keep a snapshot of the database VM for safety, forever.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-bad\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The tempting way\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>virsh snapshot-create-as db-1 before-upgrade\n# ... six months later, still there\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See what goes wrong\u003c/summary>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>$ virsh pool-info default\nAvailable: 3.2 GiB\nerror: Write error on db-1: No space left on device\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp class=\"wp-why\">\u003cstrong>Why it fails.\u003c/strong> A snapshot is not a backup. Every write after it lands in a growing overlay, reads get slower along the chain and the pool eventually fills, which pauses every guest.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"wp-more\">\u003csummary>See the way that works\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"wp-step wp-good\">\u003cp class=\"wp-tag\">The way that works\u003c/p>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode>virsh snapshot-list db-1\nvirsh snapshot-delete db-1 before-upgrade\n# use real backups for protection\nvirsh domblklist db-1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003cp>Delete snapshots when the change is verified, and take backups with a tool built for it.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating snapshots as backups\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving snapshots for weeks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Overcommitting thin disks with no alert on pool free space\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cloning a guest without changing its host name, machine id and SSH host keys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"image-formats","text":"Image formats"},{"id":"snapshots","text":"Snapshots"},{"id":"templates-and-clones","text":"Templates and clones"},{"id":"incident-the-pool-that-filled-overnight","text":"Incident: The pool that filled overnight"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"QEMU: disk images","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/system/images.html"},{"t":"libvirt: snapshots","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatsnapshot.html"},{"t":"cloud-init documentation","u":"https://cloudinit.readthedocs.io/"}],"text":"A virtual machine&#39;s disk is a file or a volume on the host. How that file is stored decides speed, space use and what happens when you take a snapshot. What you will learn Common image formats: raw, qcow2, VMDK, VHDX Thin and thick provisioning How a snapshot works and why long chains cause trouble Templates, clones and cloud-init Image formats Format Used by Notes raw KVM, many clouds Simple and fast, no features of its own qcow2 KVM and QEMU Thin provisioning, snapshots, compression VMDK VMware Thin or thick, snapshots as delta files VHDX Hyper-V Dynamic or fixed $ qemu-img info web-01.qcow2 image: web-01.qcow2 file format: qcow2 virtual size: 100 GiB (107374182400 bytes) disk size: 8.2 GiB The virtual size is what the guest sees. The disk size is what the file takes on the host. That gap is thin provisioning : space is taken only as the guest writes. It saves space and lets you promise more than you have, which is another kind of overcommit. If every guest fills its disk, the storage pool runs out and guests fail with write errors. Snapshots A snapshot freezes the disk at a point in time. New writes go to a separate file, called a delta or overlay, and the original becomes read-only. Rolling back means discarding the delta. $ virsh snapshot-create-as web-01 before-upgrade Domain snapshot before-upgrade created $ virsh snapshot-list web-01 Name Creation Time State ------------------------------------------------------ before-upgrade 2026-11-02 10:14:09 +0000 shutoff A snapshot is not a backup. It lives on the same storage as the disk, so losing the storage loses both. Each snapshot adds a layer: reads may have to look through several files, which slows the guest, and every delta uses space. A chain of dozens of snapshots can fill a pool and make deleting them slow because deltas must be merged. Use snapshots for short-lived safety, such as before an upgrade, and delete them when the change is accepted. Templates and clones A template is a prepared guest image with the operating system installed and cleaned. A full clone copies the disk. A linked clone uses the template as a read-only base and stores only the differences, so it is fast and small, but depends on the base. cloud-init configures a guest at first boot from user data: host name, users, SSH keys, packages. It lets one generic template serve many roles. Incident: The pool that filled overnight Incident A pool that was 60 percent full on Friday reached 100 percent on Sunday and every guest paused. $ virsh pool-info vmstore Capacity: 500.00 GiB Allocation: 499.98 GiB Available: 0.02 GiB $ virsh snapshot-list db-01 | wc -l 42 A backup tool created a snapshot of db-01 every hour and never removed them. Forty snapshots each held the changes of an hour of database writes. Deleting the old snapshots merged the files and freed 180 GiB, and the guests resumed. The team added a retention rule and an alert at 80 percent pool use. Worked example: a tempting wrong path Goal. Keep a snapshot of the database VM for safety, forever. The tempting way virsh snapshot-create-as db-1 before-upgrade # ... six months later, still there See what goes wrong $ virsh pool-info default Available: 3.2 GiB error: Write error on db-1: No space left on device Why it fails. A snapshot is not a backup. Every write after it lands in a growing overlay, reads get slower along the chain and the pool eventually fills, which pauses every guest. See the way that works The way that works virsh snapshot-list db-1 virsh snapshot-delete db-1 before-upgrade # use real backups for protection virsh domblklist db-1 Delete snapshots when the change is verified, and take backups with a tool built for it. Common mistakes Treating snapshots as backups Leaving snapshots for weeks Overcommitting thin disks with no alert on pool free space Cloning a guest without changing its host name, machine id and SSH host keys"},"virtualization/virtual-networking-bridges-and-vlans":{"html":"\u003cp>A guest needs a network card, and the card needs somewhere to plug in. The hypervisor provides a virtual switch for that, and the choices you make there decide who can reach the guest and what it can reach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Virtual NICs and the virtual switch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Bridged, NAT and isolated networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>VLAN tagging on the host uplink\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>SR-IOV and when to skip the virtual switch\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-virtual-switch\">The virtual switch\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each guest has one or more \u003cstrong>virtual NICs\u003c/strong>, usually virtio. On the host they appear as interfaces such as \u003ccode>vnet3\u003c/code> attached to a \u003cstrong>bridge\u003c/strong> or a vSwitch. On Linux this is often the \u003ccode>br0\u003c/code> bridge or an Open vSwitch bridge. VMware calls it a vSwitch, and Hyper-V a virtual switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ip -br link show master br0\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ip -br \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">link\u003c/span> show master br0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1      UP    3c:ec:ef:11:22:33\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet0     UP    fe:54:00:aa:bb:01\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet1     UP    fe:54:00:aa:bb:02\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The physical port \u003ccode>eno1\u003c/code> and the guest ports \u003ccode>vnet0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>vnet1\u003c/code> are on one bridge, so the guests behave like machines plugged into the same switch as the host.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"network-modes\">Network modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Bridged\u003c/strong>: guests have addresses on the physical network and are visible to it. Common for servers.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>NAT\u003c/strong>: the host hides guests behind its own address. Guests can go out, but nothing can reach them unless you forward ports. This is libvirt&#39;s default \u003ccode>virbr0\u003c/code> network, convenient for desktops and tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Isolated\u003c/strong>: guests can reach each other and the host but not the outside.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Routed\u003c/strong>: the host routes between a guest network and the physical one without translation.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"vlans\">VLANs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>VLAN\u003c/strong> splits one physical network into separate broadcast domains using a tag in the Ethernet frame. The switch port to the host is often a \u003cstrong>trunk\u003c/strong> that carries several VLANs, and the host or hypervisor assigns each guest to one. If the trunk does not allow the tag, or the guest is on the wrong tag, the guest has a link but no connectivity.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"bridge vlan show\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>bridge vlan show\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1    1 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        30\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet0   20 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet1   30 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sr-iov\">SR-IOV\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>SR-IOV\u003c/strong> lets one physical NIC present several virtual functions that are handed directly to guests. It skips the software switch, so latency and CPU use drop. The cost is flexibility: live migration and firewall rules at the host become harder.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-new-guest-with-a-link-and-no-network\">Incident: A new guest with a link and no network\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A guest came up, got no address from DHCP, and the team blamed the DHCP server.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">guest$ ip -br addr\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ens3   UP   fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456/64\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ bridge vlan show dev vnet5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vnet5   40 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">host$ bridge vlan show dev eno1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">port    vlan-id\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">eno1    1 PVID Egress Untagged\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">        30\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The guest was assigned to VLAN 40, but the uplink \u003ccode>eno1\u003c/code> only carried VLANs 20 and 30. The DHCP server was fine. After the network team added VLAN 40 to the trunk, the guest received an address. Check each hop of the path: guest, vnet port, bridge, uplink, switch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting a guest on a NAT network and then wondering why clients cannot reach it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a trunk port must allow every VLAN used\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Two guests with the same MAC address after a clone\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Disabling the firewall on the host because a guest could not connect, without checking the VLAN first\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-virtual-switch","text":"The virtual switch"},{"id":"network-modes","text":"Network modes"},{"id":"vlans","text":"VLANs"},{"id":"sr-iov","text":"SR-IOV"},{"id":"incident-a-new-guest-with-a-link-and-no-network","text":"Incident: A new guest with a link and no network"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt: networking","u":"https://libvirt.org/formatnetwork.html"},{"t":"Linux kernel: bridge","u":"https://docs.kernel.org/networking/bridge.html"},{"t":"Open vSwitch documentation","u":"https://docs.openvswitch.org/en/latest/"}],"text":"A guest needs a network card, and the card needs somewhere to plug in. The hypervisor provides a virtual switch for that, and the choices you make there decide who can reach the guest and what it can reach. What you will learn Virtual NICs and the virtual switch Bridged, NAT and isolated networks VLAN tagging on the host uplink SR-IOV and when to skip the virtual switch The virtual switch Each guest has one or more virtual NICs , usually virtio. On the host they appear as interfaces such as vnet3 attached to a bridge or a vSwitch. On Linux this is often the br0 bridge or an Open vSwitch bridge. VMware calls it a vSwitch, and Hyper-V a virtual switch. $ ip -br link show master br0 eno1 UP 3c:ec:ef:11:22:33 vnet0 UP fe:54:00:aa:bb:01 vnet1 UP fe:54:00:aa:bb:02 The physical port eno1 and the guest ports vnet0 and vnet1 are on one bridge, so the guests behave like machines plugged into the same switch as the host. Network modes Bridged : guests have addresses on the physical network and are visible to it. Common for servers. NAT : the host hides guests behind its own address. Guests can go out, but nothing can reach them unless you forward ports. This is libvirt&#39;s default virbr0 network, convenient for desktops and tests. Isolated : guests can reach each other and the host but not the outside. Routed : the host routes between a guest network and the physical one without translation. VLANs A VLAN splits one physical network into separate broadcast domains using a tag in the Ethernet frame. The switch port to the host is often a trunk that carries several VLANs, and the host or hypervisor assigns each guest to one. If the trunk does not allow the tag, or the guest is on the wrong tag, the guest has a link but no connectivity. $ bridge vlan show port vlan-id eno1 1 PVID Egress Untagged 20 30 vnet0 20 PVID Egress Untagged vnet1 30 PVID Egress Untagged SR-IOV SR-IOV lets one physical NIC present several virtual functions that are handed directly to guests. It skips the software switch, so latency and CPU use drop. The cost is flexibility: live migration and firewall rules at the host become harder. Incident: A new guest with a link and no network Incident A guest came up, got no address from DHCP, and the team blamed the DHCP server. guest$ ip -br addr ens3 UP fe80::5054:ff:fe12:3456/64 host$ bridge vlan show dev vnet5 port vlan-id vnet5 40 PVID Egress Untagged host$ bridge vlan show dev eno1 port vlan-id eno1 1 PVID Egress Untagged 20 30 The guest was assigned to VLAN 40, but the uplink eno1 only carried VLANs 20 and 30. The DHCP server was fine. After the network team added VLAN 40 to the trunk, the guest received an address. Check each hop of the path: guest, vnet port, bridge, uplink, switch. Common mistakes Putting a guest on a NAT network and then wondering why clients cannot reach it Forgetting that a trunk port must allow every VLAN used Two guests with the same MAC address after a clone Disabling the firewall on the host because a guest could not connect, without checking the VLAN first"},"virtualization/live-migration-ha-and-troubleshooting":{"html":"\u003cp>A host needs patching, or fails. The point of a virtual platform is that guests do not have to suffer for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Live migration and what it needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>High availability, fencing and split brain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Capacity planning for failure (N+1)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist for the faults you will meet\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"live-migration\">Live migration\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Live migration\u003c/strong> (VMware vMotion, KVM live migration) copies a running guest&#39;s memory from one host to another while it keeps running, then switches over in a short pause of tens of milliseconds. It makes host maintenance invisible to users.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Requirements usually include:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A shared or replicated storage that both hosts can reach, or storage migration that copies the disk too\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Compatible CPU features: a guest that uses a new instruction cannot move to an older CPU, so clusters set a common \u003cstrong>CPU model\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A fast network between hosts for the memory copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Matching networks on both hosts, so the guest keeps its connectivity\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\nvirsh list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\"> Id   Name   State\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">-------------------\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A guest that dirties memory faster than the network can copy it may never converge. Throttling the guest, or using post-copy migration, solves that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"high-availability\">High availability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>HA\u003c/strong> restarts guests on another host after a host failure. It differs from migration: the guest does restart, with a short outage. The cluster must be sure the failed host is really off before it starts the same guest elsewhere, otherwise two copies write to one disk and corrupt it. That is the danger of \u003cstrong>split brain\u003c/strong>. \u003cstrong>Fencing\u003c/strong> (also called STONITH) cuts power or access to a suspect host to make sure it is down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A cluster also needs \u003cstrong>quorum\u003c/strong>: a majority of nodes must agree on who is alive. With two nodes and no tie-breaker, a network split leaves each side unsure.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-for-failure\">Plan for failure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spare capacity must exist for the guests that restart. \u003cstrong>N+1\u003c/strong> means the cluster can lose one host and still run everything. If every host runs at 85 percent memory, a failed host leaves guests with nowhere to go.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"troubleshooting-checklist\">Troubleshooting checklist\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Is hardware virtualization on, and is \u003ccode>kvm\u003c/code> loaded?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there free memory on the host for the guest, with overcommit counted?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is there free space in the storage pool, including snapshot deltas?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the disk file present, with the right permissions and SELinux or AppArmor labels?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Is the guest on the right network and VLAN?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Are guests waiting for CPU (steal or ready time)?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -u libvirtd --since &quot;10 min ago&quot; | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u libvirtd --since \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;10 min ago&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libvirtd[1043]: internal error: qemu unexpectedly closed the monitor\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">libvirtd[1043]: cannot set up guest memory 'pc.ram': Cannot allocate memory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-maintenance-that-moved-the-problem\">Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Draining a host for patching started a chain of guest failures on the next host.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory\nvirsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | wc -l\nvirsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Memory size:         134217728 KiB\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">19\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>virsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: internal error: process exited while connecting to monitor: Cannot allocate memory\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Host B already used almost all of its memory. The migration failed, and then the HA service on host B restarted two guests during the same minute and exhausted memory. The team set a rule that a host may hold at most 80 percent of its memory with guests, so one host can always be drained into the rest, and drained one host at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running clusters at high memory use and calling it efficient\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing CPU generations without a common CPU model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>HA with no fencing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a snapshot as the way to move a guest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"live-migration","text":"Live migration"},{"id":"high-availability","text":"High availability"},{"id":"plan-for-failure","text":"Plan for failure"},{"id":"troubleshooting-checklist","text":"Troubleshooting checklist"},{"id":"incident-maintenance-that-moved-the-problem","text":"Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"libvirt: migration","u":"https://libvirt.org/migration.html"},{"t":"QEMU: live migration","u":"https://www.qemu.org/docs/master/devel/migration/main.html"},{"t":"Proxmox VE documentation","u":"https://pve.proxmox.com/pve-docs/"}],"text":"A host needs patching, or fails. The point of a virtual platform is that guests do not have to suffer for it. What you will learn Live migration and what it needs High availability, fencing and split brain Capacity planning for failure (N+1) A checklist for the faults you will meet Live migration Live migration (VMware vMotion, KVM live migration) copies a running guest&#39;s memory from one host to another while it keeps running, then switches over in a short pause of tens of milliseconds. It makes host maintenance invisible to users. Requirements usually include: A shared or replicated storage that both hosts can reach, or storage migration that copies the disk too Compatible CPU features: a guest that uses a new instruction cannot move to an older CPU, so clusters set a common CPU model A fast network between hosts for the memory copy Matching networks on both hosts, so the guest keeps its connectivity $ virsh migrate --live --persistent web-01 qemu+ssh://host-b/system $ virsh list Id Name State ------------------- A guest that dirties memory faster than the network can copy it may never converge. Throttling the guest, or using post-copy migration, solves that. High availability HA restarts guests on another host after a host failure. It differs from migration: the guest does restart, with a short outage. The cluster must be sure the failed host is really off before it starts the same guest elsewhere, otherwise two copies write to one disk and corrupt it. That is the danger of split brain . Fencing (also called STONITH) cuts power or access to a suspect host to make sure it is down. A cluster also needs quorum : a majority of nodes must agree on who is alive. With two nodes and no tie-breaker, a network split leaves each side unsure. Plan for failure Spare capacity must exist for the guests that restart. N+1 means the cluster can lose one host and still run everything. If every host runs at 85 percent memory, a failed host leaves guests with nowhere to go. Troubleshooting checklist Is hardware virtualization on, and is kvm loaded? Is there free memory on the host for the guest, with overcommit counted? Is there free space in the storage pool, including snapshot deltas? Is the disk file present, with the right permissions and SELinux or AppArmor labels? Is the guest on the right network and VLAN? Are guests waiting for CPU (steal or ready time)? $ journalctl -u libvirtd --since \"10 min ago\" | tail -3 libvirtd[1043]: internal error: qemu unexpectedly closed the monitor libvirtd[1043]: cannot set up guest memory 'pc.ram': Cannot allocate memory Incident: Maintenance that moved the problem Incident Draining a host for patching started a chain of guest failures on the next host. $ virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system nodeinfo | grep Memory Memory size: 134217728 KiB $ virsh -c qemu+ssh://host-b/system list | wc -l 19 $ virsh migrate --live batch-07 qemu+ssh://host-b/system error: internal error: process exited while connecting to monitor: Cannot allocate memory Host B already used almost all of its memory. The migration failed, and then the HA service on host B restarted two guests during the same minute and exhausted memory. The team set a rule that a host may hold at most 80 percent of its memory with guests, so one host can always be drained into the rest, and drained one host at a time. Common mistakes Running clusters at high memory use and calling it efficient Mixing CPU generations without a common CPU model HA with no fencing Using a snapshot as the way to move a guest"}});